# decoder_mp3.c/minimp3.h reference this directly (see decoder_cfg.h and
# tuya_mem_section.h), so it stays global rather than nested under
# ENABLE_AI_PLAYER's Decoder Options menu in src/audio_player/Kconfig; no other
# platform selects it.
config MP3_DECODER_STATIC_BUF
    bool "MP3_DECODER_STATIC_BUF: allocate minimp3 scratch/context from a dedicated internal-RAM pool"
    default n
    help
      Route the decoder's MP3_MALLOC/MP3_FREE hook (src/audio_player/.../minimp3.h)
      to a small dedicated internal-RAM pool (mcu/src/mp3_internal_pool.c, ~23KB)
      instead of the general purpose heap.

      This is about memory latency, not heap pressure. With ENABLE_EXT_RAM the
      MP3_MALLOC hook allocates from PSRAM, and decoding out of PSRAM cannot keep
      up: mp3dec_scratch_t (grbuf/syn, ~16KB) is read and written throughout every
      frame, which stutters audibly on SiWx917. The pool sits in internal RAM,
      removing the PSRAM access latency.

      It is a dedicated pool rather than the shared FreeRTOS heap
      (pvPortMalloc/ucHeap) on purpose: that heap also serves task stacks and
      queues, and routing the decoder through it was tried and failed on real
      hardware -- a 16KB allocation returned NULL because nothing that size was
      free. This pool never competes with anything else.

      Costs ~23KB of internal RAM permanently (reserved for the pool, not
      freed back even when idle) and only ever holds one context + one
      scratch buffer at a time, so enable it only for single-stream playback.

